A capability is one named action with a contract and a server-side handler.
A contract defines the shared input, output, failures, and metadata.
Effect Schema provides types and runtime checks for these values. `defineContract` owns the name, Effect schemas, annotations, and approval setting. `implement` binds that contract to its handler.

Here, a surface is an interface for calling the action.
A projection converts registered capabilities into an interface.
The interfaces are command line, HTTP, MCP tools, browser RPC, and code mode.
Each interface derives its input and failure definitions from the same schemas.

The split replaced `defineCapability`, which bundled contract data and its handler in one definition. The RPC client needed the contract without importing server implementation. `toRpcGroup` now projects contracts alone; `toRpc` connects those same contracts to implemented capabilities. The browser docs client imports only the shared contracts and RPC group. [Commit 9a7bea5](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/commit/9a7bea5) records the split.

To add a capability, define its contract and implement it beside the data or infrastructure it owns. Register it in `capabilities`, then check each interface you keep. `packages/capability/test/` exercises projections; `apps/mischief/src/capabilities/index.ts` shows the hosted registry.

Michael Arnaldi demonstrates Schema decoding request input and encoding a frontend-safe result while preserving its output type. [Effect Meetup SF, 27:17](https://youtu.be/BqUnsDnMnMo?t=1637). In another conversation, the speakers discuss generated schemas and migrations as work agents can maintain, and a rule that rejects ad hoc JSON parsing. [Cause & Effect 10, 63:49](https://youtu.be/irmz5X3c7Do?t=3829) [67:53](https://youtu.be/irmz5X3c7Do?t=4073).

[Analytics](/systems/analytics) shows a separate infrastructure cartridge; [interest signup](/systems/interest) shows core ports with a delivery adapter outside core. Neither turns static pages or protocol discovery into domain capabilities.

See [lifecycles are machines](/lore/lifecycles-are-machines) for stateful handlers and [cartridges](/lore/cartridges) for the infrastructure boundary. [HATEOAS](/lore/hateoas) covers links that guide an agent's next action; [hexagonal architecture](/lore/hexagonal-architecture) names the ports-and-adapters principle.
